# data/ is server-side only. Nothing here is fetched over HTTP.
#
# atlas.php and its siblings read these files from disk (__DIR__ . '/data/...')
# and inline the result into the page, so denying the directory costs nothing.
#
# It is denied because on 2026-09-01 data/subscribers.txt was serving HTTP 200
# to anyone who asked — 9 real subscriber names and email addresses, readable
# since February. The site-wide rules could not have caught it: od9's .htaccess
# is deliberately narrow on .txt and .json because it serves llms.txt,
# robots.txt and agent.json, so the fix has to be scoped to the directory that
# holds data rather than to the extension.
#
# What made it invisible was worse than the file itself. data/*.json answered
# 403, so the directory looked protected — but that 403 came from CLOUDFLARE
# (4.5KB error page), not from this origin (Apache's is ~358 bytes). An edge
# rule we do not own, that can be changed or bypassed by reaching the origin
# directly, was standing in for a permission we had never actually set. Same
# lesson as the August webroot source-disclosure sweep: "it returns 403" is not
# the same claim as "we deny it".
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require all denied
</IfModule>
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Order Deny,Allow
    Deny from all
</IfModule>
